nio: declare no system mapper, so memory allocation can proceed

Run 589 reached bsd_init -- the matching no longer holds the boot
thread -- and then stopped at a wait with a backtrace:/n
    waitForSystemMapper

IOMapper::gSystem starts as kUnknown.  checkForSystemMapper()
(called from the memory-allocation path) waits while that value is
in the wait state, until a driver calls setMapperRequired(false) to
declare that the platform has no IOMMU.  This board has no IOMMU and
no driver here makes that call, so the first kmem_suballoc() in
bsd_init sleeps for ever and prints the wait and its backtrace.

Set the initial value to kNoMapper instead.  That is the state a
platform without an IOMMU is supposed to declare: allocations then
use physical mappings directly and never wait.  One line, at the
definition of the static, before any allocation can ask.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOMapper.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOMapper.cpp
index 39ab332..bf0f763 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOMapper.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOMapper.cpp
@@ -60,7 +60,12 @@ OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOMapper, 13);
 OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOMapper, 14);
 OSMetaClassDefineReservedUnused(IOMapper, 15);
 
-IOMapper * IOMapper::gSystem = (IOMapper *) IOMapper::kUnknown;
+/* nio: this board has no IOMMU.  The default kUnknown makes
+ * checkForSystemMapper() wait for a driver to declare a system
+ * mapper or none; no driver here ever does, so the first
+ * kmem_suballoc() in bsd_init sleeps forever in waitForSystemMapper
+ * (run 589 shows the wait and its backtrace).  Declare none up front. */
+IOMapper * IOMapper::gSystem = (IOMapper *) IOMapper::kNoMapper;
 
 class IOMapperLock {
 	IOLock *fWaitLock;
